WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.980
Welcome to ValiStera Academy. In this

2
00:00:02.980 --> 00:00:05.500
lesson, we will explore
the fundamentals of

3
00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:09.560
good manufacturing practice,
commonly known as GMP.

4
00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:13.940
Pharmaceutical manufacturing
requires more than producing a

5
00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:17.460
medicine that passes
laboratory testing. It requires

6
00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:22.040
controlled processes, trained
personnel, suitable equipment, and

7
00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:23.300
reliable documentation.

8
00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:26.080
By the end of this lesson, you

9
00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:29.180
will understand the purpose of GMP, its

10
00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:31.600
core principles and how it contributes to

11
00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:34.460
patient safety and
consistent product quality.

12
00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:38.080
Every pharmaceutical product
is intended to deliver

13
00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:42.080
a specific therapeutic
benefit. However, failures in

14
00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:45.340
manufacturing controls
can create serious risks.

15
00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:49.820
These risks may include
contamination, incorrect strength,

16
00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:54.160
product mix-ups, or
inconsistent performance. Many

17
00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:57.680
manufacturing defects cannot
be identified simply by

18
00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:00.140
testing a small sample
of the finished batch.

19
00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:03.420
That is why pharmaceutical quality must be

20
00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:08.340
systematically managed throughout
manufacturing, testing, packaging and

21
00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:12.480
storage. GMP provides
essential controls to reduce

22
00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:14.740
these risks and protect patients.

23
00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:20.040
GMP stands for Good
Manufacturing Practice. It

24
00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:22.020
is a system of principles and controls

25
00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:25.620
intended to ensure that
pharmaceutical products are

26
00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:29.480
consistently manufactured and
controlled according to appropriate

27
00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:30.880
quality standards.

28
00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:35.280
GMP covers activities such
as personnel training,

29
00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:40.720
equipment maintenance, cleaning,
manufacturing operations, quality control

30
00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:44.580
and documentation. For example,
a pharmaceutical mixing

31
00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:50.440
vessel should be suitable for its intended purpose,
properly maintained and cleaned according to

32
00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:51.640
approved procedures.

33
00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:55.360
GMP is not a single test or

34
00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:58.120
certificate. It is an ongoing system of

35
00:01:58.120 --> 00:02:00.800
controlled operations
and quality oversight.

36
00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:05.380
A pharmaceutical quality
system, or PQS, brings

37
00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:11.280
together the arrangements used to manage
product quality. GMP is an essential part of

38
00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:11.980
this system.

39
00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:15.840
Other important elements
include quality risk management,

40
00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:20.360
change management, corrective and
preventive actions, management

41
00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:24.900
responsibilities, and continual
improvement. For example, if

42
00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:27.880
a recurring equipment
problem is identified, the

43
00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:31.660
quality system should support
investigation, appropriate corrective

44
00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:35.400
action, and evaluation of
whether the action was effective.

45
00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:39.140
An effective quality system
is therefore proactive

46
00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:40.520
as well as reactive.

47
00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:44.400
One of the most important GMP principles

48
00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:47.620
is that finished product
testing alone cannot

49
00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:51.320
provide complete assurance
of quality. Consider a

50
00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:54.660
manufacturing process with
inadequate mixing controls.

51
00:02:55.480 --> 00:02:57.680
Even if the finished product samples meet

52
00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:00.960
their specifications,
that result alone may not

53
00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:08.240
demonstrate that every part of the batch was manufactured
consistently. GMP, therefore, emphasizes suitable

54
00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:12.420
process design, defined
operating parameters, in-process

55
00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:15.640
controls, monitoring and
documented verification.

56
00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:19.540
Testing remains essential,
but it works together

57
00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:22.920
with manufacturing controls
rather than replacing them.

58
00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:30.640
People play a critical role in pharmaceutical quality.
Personnel must have appropriate qualifications, training

59
00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:33.540
and experience for their
assigned responsibilities.

60
00:03:34.420 --> 00:03:38.540
Training should cover relevant
GMP principles, approved

61
00:03:38.540 --> 00:03:43.240
procedures, and specific
operational activities. For example,

62
00:03:43.540 --> 00:03:46.500
an operator responsible for
equipment cleaning should

63
00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:50.240
understand the approved cleaning
procedure, required precautions,

64
00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:52.100
and associated documentation.

65
00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:56.800
Training effectiveness and
continuing competence should be

66
00:03:56.800 --> 00:04:00.060
managed according to applicable
requirements and company

67
00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:03.440
procedures. Having a
training record does not

68
00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:06.140
automatically demonstrate
that a person can perform

69
00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:08.320
every assigned activity correctly.

70
00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:14.180
Pharmaceutical manufacturing requires
suitable premises, equipment and

71
00:04:14.180 --> 00:04:17.800
supporting utilities. Facilities
should be designed and

72
00:04:17.800 --> 00:04:21.060
maintained to support
controlled operations and reduce

73
00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:23.400
contamination or mix-up risks.

74
00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:30.000
Equipment should be appropriate for its intended
use and subject to relevant maintenance, calibration

75
00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:34.640
and qualification activities.
Supporting utilities, such as

76
00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:39.080
pharmaceutical water, compressed
gases, and HVAC systems,

77
00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:42.360
may also require defined
controls depending on

78
00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:44.840
their intended use and associated risks.

79
00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:49.960
Equipment qualification helps
provide documented evidence that

80
00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:52.000
a system is suitable for its intended

81
00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:55.400
purpose. We will explore
qualification in detail

82
00:04:55.400 --> 00:04:56.740
in later lessons.

83
00:04:57.560 --> 00:05:01.560
Documentation is fundamental
to GMP because it

84
00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:04.940
communicates instructions and
provides evidence of performed

85
00:05:04.940 --> 00:05:09.700
activities. Standard operating
procedures describe how defined

86
00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:11.880
activities should be carried out.

87
00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:15.780
Records demonstrate what was
actually performed, observed,

88
00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:20.280
and reviewed. GMP documentation
should be accurate,

89
00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:24.880
attributable, legible, contemporaneous,
and appropriately retained.

90
00:05:25.900 --> 00:05:28.680
For example, when an
equipment qualification test

91
00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:31.340
is executed, the results
should be recorded

92
00:05:31.340 --> 00:05:34.140
in accordance with the
approved protocol and

93
00:05:34.140 --> 00:05:39.640
applicable documentation procedures.
Reliable records support traceability,

94
00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:42.240
investigations, and quality decisions.

95
00:05:43.180 --> 00:05:47.280
Manufacturing and quality control
have complementary responsibilities.

96
00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:52.220
Manufacturing personnel carry out
production activities according

97
00:05:52.220 --> 00:05:55.660
to approved instructions and
defined process controls.

98
00:05:56.340 --> 00:06:00.280
Quality control performs
sampling, testing, and other

99
00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:04.280
laboratory-related activities
according to established procedures.

100
00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:08.320
The pharmaceutical quality
system also includes independent

101
00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:11.880
quality oversight and defined
responsibilities for reviewing

102
00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:16.540
records, evaluating deviations
and authorizing quality decisions.

103
00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:19.300
A batch should not be released merely

104
00:06:19.400 --> 00:06:22.860
because a laboratory
result is acceptable. The

105
00:06:22.860 --> 00:06:27.280
applicable manufacturing, documentation
and release requirements must

106
00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:28.380
also be satisfied.

107
00:06:29.580 --> 00:06:33.360
Even well-controlled pharmaceutical
operations can experience

108
00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:37.100
unexpected events. A
deviation occurs when an

109
00:06:37.100 --> 00:06:39.800
activity or result
differs from an applicable

110
00:06:39.800 --> 00:06:43.040
approved requirement or
established expectation.

111
00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:47.260
Deviations should be documented
and evaluated according

112
00:06:47.260 --> 00:06:50.880
to their potential impact.
Where appropriate, an

113
00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:55.300
investigation identifies contributing
factors and supports corrective

114
00:06:55.300 --> 00:06:56.780
or preventive actions.

115
00:06:57.760 --> 00:07:00.700
Change control provides
a structured approach for

116
00:07:00.700 --> 00:07:05.180
evaluating proposed changes before
implementation, including their

117
00:07:05.180 --> 00:07:08.140
potential effect on product
quality and validated

118
00:07:08.140 --> 00:07:12.660
status. These systems help
organizations maintain control

119
00:07:12.660 --> 00:07:14.480
and improve their processes.

120
00:07:15.300 --> 00:07:18.700
Consider a pharmaceutical
manufacturing vessel prepared for

121
00:07:18.700 --> 00:07:22.080
production. Before use,
personnel may need to

122
00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:26.380
verify the equipment's identification,
cleaning status, calibration

123
00:07:26.380 --> 00:07:29.620
status of relevant
instruments and readiness for

124
00:07:29.620 --> 00:07:33.440
the intended manufacturing
operation. The applicable checks

125
00:07:33.440 --> 00:07:37.260
depend on the equipment,
process and approved procedures.

126
00:07:37.860 --> 00:07:40.860
Now imagine that a
critical temperature sensor

127
00:07:40.920 --> 00:07:44.180
is found outside its
calibration validity period.

128
00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:47.260
The appropriate response is not simply to

129
00:07:47.260 --> 00:07:48.260
continue production.

130
00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:54.580
The situation should be assessed through the relevant
quality procedures to determine the equipment

131
00:07:54.580 --> 00:07:57.520
suitability for use and
any potential product

132
00:07:57.520 --> 00:08:01.900
quality impact. This
example demonstrates how GMP

133
00:08:01.900 --> 00:08:04.220
principles guide everyday decisions.

134
00:08:05.080 --> 00:08:06.940
Let us review the main points from

135
00:08:06.940 --> 00:08:09.540
this lesson. GMP supports
the consistent manufacture

136
00:08:10.780 --> 00:08:15.180
and control of pharmaceutical
products. Effective GMP

137
00:08:15.180 --> 00:08:18.640
depends on trained personnel,
suitable facilities and

138
00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:23.140
equipment, controlled procedures,
reliable documentation, and an

139
00:08:23.140 --> 00:08:24.600
effective quality system.

140
00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:27.920
Product quality cannot
be assured by finished

141
00:08:27.920 --> 00:08:31.500
product testing alone.
Every person involved in

142
00:08:31.500 --> 00:08:35.700
pharmaceutical operations contributes
to maintaining quality and

143
00:08:35.700 --> 00:08:36.700
protecting patients.

144
00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:39.600
In our next lesson, we will explore

145
00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:42.780
the pharmaceutical quality
system and good documentation

146
00:08:42.780 --> 00:08:46.670
practices in greater detail. Thank you
for learning with ValiStera Academy.
